course: biology
lesson: 386
title: Giants people saw: Steller's sea cow, Haast's eagle, giant lemurs and elephant birds
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In 1741 sailors stranded on cold islands in the far north Pacific saw huge gentle animals grazing on kelp near the shore, up to about eight metres long. They were Steller's sea cows, and only about 27 years later they were all gone.
explain: Islands often had giants that no one else had: Haast's eagle hunting moa in New Zealand, giant lemurs and elephant birds in Madagascar.
explain: These animals had never met human hunters, so they did not run away. They were big and slow, had few babies, and could not recover when people hunted them, brought rats and dogs and cut the forests.
explain: People saw these giants with their own eyes, and some old stories, like the Māori stories of a huge bird called Pouakai, may remember them.
explain: Today people try to protect island animals like the kakapo with guards and predator-free islands, so the same thing does not happen again.
fact: Steller's sea cow was found in 1741 near islands in the far north Pacific, was up to about eight metres long and ate kelp.
fact: The sea cow could not dive deep and was too slow to escape, and hunters killed them all within about 27 years.
fact: Steller's sea cow had no teeth; hard rough plates in its mouth crushed soft seaweed, and its living relatives are the manatee and the dugong.
fact: Haast's eagle of New Zealand was the biggest eagle known; it hunted moa, giant flightless birds much heavier than itself, with claws as big as a tiger's.
fact: Haast's eagle died out about 600 years ago, soon after people hunted the moa away.
fact: Many people think Māori stories of a huge bird called Pouakai remember Haast's eagle; stories can keep real memories but also change as they are retold.
fact: Madagascar had giant lemurs, some as heavy as a grown man or heavier, and some hung upside down in trees like sloths.
fact: Elephant birds of Madagascar were taller than a grown-up, much heavier than an ostrich, could not fly and died out roughly a thousand years ago.
fact: One elephant bird egg could hold about as much as 150 chicken eggs, and pieces of its thick shell are still found on beaches and dunes.
fact: Moa had three forward toes and a small back toe, while an ostrich has only two toes on each foot, the fewest of any bird.
fact: Island giants often had no fear of people, and hunting, new animals like rats and dogs and cut forests hit them all together.
exercise: When was Steller's sea cow found by scientists? | In 1741 | In the year 1066 | In 1990, quite recently | Two million years ago
exercise: What did Steller's sea cow eat? | Kelp, a big seaweed | Fish and young baby seals | Grass growing on the shore | Clams and crabs on the seabed
exercise: How long after it was described was the sea cow gone? | About 27 years | About 2,700 years | Only about 2 years | It is still alive
exercise: What did the sea cow have instead of teeth? | Hard rough plates for crushing seaweed | Sharp fangs | A long beak like a turtle's | Rows of baleen like the biggest whales
exercise: Which living animals are the sea cow's relatives? | The manatee and the dugong | The walrus and the seal | The cow and the bull | The dolphin and the orca
exercise: What did Haast's eagle hunt? | Moa, giant flightless birds | Mice and beetles | Fish from the sea near the coast | Sheep on farms
exercise: How big were Haast's eagle's claws? | As big as a tiger's claws | As small as a sparrow's | As big as a car door | It had no claws at all
exercise: Why did Haast's eagle die out? | People hunted away the moa it ate | It was too cold in New Zealand in winter | It flew away to Australia | Lions ate it
exercise: What is Pouakai? | A huge bird in Māori stories | A kind of lemur | A sea cow's song | A volcano on the North Island of New Zealand
exercise: Where did giant lemurs live? | Madagascar | New Zealand | The island of Ireland | Northern Canada
exercise: Why are some giant lemurs called sloth lemurs? | They hung upside down in trees like sloths | They were green like moss | They slept for years | They were close relatives of the sloths of South America
exercise: How did elephant birds get about? | They walked, as they could not fly | They flew over the sea to Africa | They swam like penguins | They rode on elephants
exercise: How much could one elephant bird egg hold? | About as much as 150 chicken eggs | About as much as 2 chicken eggs | About as much as a cup of tea | About as much as a bathtub
exercise: Why do pieces of elephant bird eggshell still turn up? | The shells were thick and hard and last a long time in sand | Elephant birds still lay eggs | People make fake ones to sell to the tourists who visit Madagascar | The eggs grow in the sand
exercise: How many toes does an ostrich have on each foot? | Two | Three | Four | Five
exercise: Why did island giants not run from people? | They had never met human hunters before | They were far too brave to run from anything | They liked people's food and came too close | They could not see well with their small eyes
test: «sea cows are just cows that swim lol» What is right? | It was a huge sea animal related to manatees, not a cow | Yes, they were farm cows that learned to swim in the sea | It was a kind of whale shark | It was a big seal
test: Count them: a moa footprint shows 3 forward toes. How many forward toes on both feet of 2 moa? | Twelve | Six | Three | Eight
test: Count them: 2 ostriches. How many toes in all? | Eight | Four | Twelve | Six
test: «eagle eating a bird bigger than itself? no way» What is right? | Haast's eagle hunted moa much heavier than itself | Eagles only ever eat mice and other small animals | Moa were tiny like chickens | It ate moa eggs only
test: «egg bigger than a rugby ball, how many brekkies is that» About how many chicken eggs' worth? | About 150 | Only about 5 | About 1,500 | Only about 15
test: What would have happened if the sea cows had been protected in 1741? | They might still be alive today | They would have turned into whales | Nothing could have saved them anyway | They would have flown away
test: In the Madagascar cartoon films King Julien is a lively little lemur. A friend says all lemurs were always small. What is right? | Some long-gone lemurs were as heavy as a grown man or heavier | Lemurs were never bigger than a cat | Lemurs are a kind of squirrel | Giant lemurs still live in every zoo in Madagascar and in Europe
test: «the Pouakai story is just a fairy tale, nothing real» What is the fair view? | It may remember the real Haast's eagle, though stories change | It proves dragons were real | It is about a sea cow | It was written last year by a children's author to sell books
test: «we'll bring our dog to the kakapo island, he's chill» Why is that a bad idea? | Dogs and rats can kill island birds that never met them | Kakapo eat dogs | Dogs get seasick on islands | Kakapo are too loud for dogs, who would bark at them all night and day
test: Why are big, slow animals often the first to vanish when things change? | They have few babies and cannot escape or recover fast | They eat too little | They are invisible to hunters | They live too long, so they get old and tired and stop eating
test: Which three things together hit island giants after people arrived? | Hunting, new animals like rats and dogs, and cut forests | Snow, earthquakes and floods | Volcanoes, meteors and comets | Too much fruit, too much rain and too much sun
exam: Ascertain the approximate interval between the scientific description of Steller's sea cow and its extinction. | Twenty-seven years | Two hundred and seventy years | Two years | Two thousand seven hundred years
exam: Identify the principal prey of Haast's eagle. | The moa, a flightless bird of great mass | Small rodents of the forest floor of New Zealand | Marine fish | Domestic sheep
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the dentition of Steller's sea cow. | It possessed no teeth, employing rough horny plates to crush seaweed | It possessed long fangs for seizing fish | It possessed a beak like that of a turtle | It possessed teeth identical in every respect to those of the manatee and the dugong
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the vulnerability of island giants to the arrival of human beings? | Lacking fear of people, they were exposed to hunting, introduced predators and loss of forest | They were too fast to be caught | They had long experience of human hunters | They reproduced so rapidly that hunting by people had no effect whatsoever upon their numbers
